## Why This Is Asked
Tests mastery of non-trivial bit interleaving without loops, a common low-level optimization in graphics/encoding.

## Key Concepts
- Bit spreading (Morton order)
- Mask-and-shift interleaving
- 128-bit composition from two 64-bit halves

## Code Example
```c
// Pseudocode outline (no branches)
uint128 interleaveBits128(uint128 x){
  uint128 r;
  uint64_t lo = x.lo, hi = x.hi;
  uint64_t sLo = spreadBy2(lo); // using masks 0x5555.., 0x3333.., etc.
  uint64_t sHi = spreadBy2(hi);
  r.lo = sLo;
  r.hi = sHi << 1;
  return r;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests advanced 128-bit bit-twiddling, boundary shifts, and correctness under rotation.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit values represented as hi/lo 64-bit halves
- Rotation across a 64-bit boundary
- Handling n in [0,127] with minimal branching in production

## Code Example
```javascript
uint128 rotateLeft128(uint128 x, unsigned n){
  n &= 127; // modulo 128
  if (n < 64){
    uint64_t hi = (x.hi << n) | (x.lo >> (64 - n));
    uint64_t lo = (x.lo << n) | (x.hi >> (64 - n));
    return (uint128){.hi = hi, .lo = lo};
  } else {
    unsigned k = n - 64;
    uint64_t hi = (x.lo << k) | (x.hi >> (64 - k));
    uint64_t lo = (x.hi << k) | (x.lo >> (64 - k));
    return (uint128){.hi = hi, .lo = lo};
  }
}
```

